Name: export audioClip

Type: command

Syntax: export <audioClip> to {file <filePath> | <container>} [as {wave | aiff | ulaw}]

Syntax: export <videoClip> to {file <filePath> | <container>}

Summary:
Writes an <audioClip> back out as a WAV, AIFF or AU sound file, or a
<videoClip> as the file it was imported from.

Introduced: 9.7.1

OS: mac, windows, linux, android, html5

Platforms: desktop, server, mobile

Security: disk

Example:
export audioClip "Boing" to file "Boing.wav"

Example:
export audioClip 1 of stack "Old HyperCard Stack" to file "Harpsichord.aiff"

Example:
local tSound
export audioClip "Chime" to tSound as aiff
put tSound into URL ("binfile:" & specialFolderPath("desktop") & "/Chime.aif")

Example:
export videoClip "Intro" to file "Intro.mov"

Parameters:
audioClip:
A chunk reference that begins with the word audioClip (or ac), such as
audioClip "Boing" or audioClip 2 of stack "Sounds".

videoClip:
A chunk reference that begins with the word videoClip (or vc).

filePath:
The name and location of the file to write. If you specify a name but
not a location, the file is written to the <defaultFolder>.

container:
A variable, or any other container, that is to hold the file's bytes.

Description:
Use the <export audioClip> command to get a sound back out of a stack:
one imported with <import>, or one the HyperCard importer made from a
stack's sound resources.

An <audioClip> keeps only the samples of the file it was imported from,
not the file itself. <export audioClip> writes them in a sound file of
its own, with the header a sound player needs: WAV (as wave), AIFF (as
aiff) or Sun's AU (as ulaw, the name the engine has always given that
format). Without as, a file whose name ends in ".aif", ".aiff" or
".aifc" is written as AIFF, one that ends in ".au" or ".snd" as AU, and
any other as WAV; a <container> gets a WAV file.

The file has the clip's rate, channels and sample size (8, 16 or 24-bit).
A clip that keeps mu-law samples stays mu-law in an AU file and becomes
16-bit samples in a WAV or AIFF file.

A <videoClip> keeps the whole file it was imported from, so
<export videoClip|export videoClip> writes those bytes, in whatever
format they are; name the file with that format's extension.

If the clip cannot be written (a sample size the engine does not keep,
or a format other than wave, aiff or ulaw), nothing is written and
<the result> says why. If the file cannot be opened, an error is thrown,
as for an image.

>*Note:* These forms are new in OXT-Beyond 9.7.1. In earlier versions
> (and in LiveCode) a line that names an audioClip or videoClip after
> export does not compile.

References: export (command), import (command), play (command),
audioClip (object), videoClip (object), defaultFolder (property),
the result (function)

Tags: multimedia
